MessageText:
To provide a clear answer to your original request for sample C# code generating the Fibonacci sequence:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class FibonacciSequenceGenerator
{
    public static IEnumerable<int> GenerateFibonacci(int count)
    {
        yield return 0;
        if (count > 1) yield return 1;

        int a = 0, b = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i < count; i++)
        {
            yield return a + b;
            (a, b) = (b, a + b);
        }
    }

    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        foreach (var num in GenerateFibonacci(10))
            Console.WriteLine(num); // Prints the first 10 numbers of the Fibonacci sequence
    }
}
```
This code defines a method `GenerateFibonacci` that generates the Fibonacci sequence up to the specified count. The `Main` method demonstrates how to use this generator by printing the first 10 numbers in the sequence.

To answer your original question: "in c++ ?"

Here's an example of how you can generate the Fibonacci sequence in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

class FibonacciSequenceGenerator {
public:
    void generate(int count) {
        int a = 0, b = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= count; ++i) {
            std::cout << a << " ";
            int temp = a + b;
            a = b;
            b = temp;
        }
    }
};

int main() {
    FibonacciSequenceGenerator generator;
    generator.generate(10);
    return 0;
}
```

This C++ code uses a class to encapsulate the Fibonacci sequence generation and prints the first 10 numbers of the sequence.

